# Try it locally

Use the read-only local configuration to explore the catalogue without creating
identity-provider accounts. You need Git, Ruby 4.0.9 and Bundler. The repository
also supplies a mise configuration for the supported runtime.

## Start the catalogue

Clone the public repository, open its directory and run these commands in Fish:

```fish
git clone https://github.com/damacus/release-mirror.git
cd release-mirror
mise trust
mise install
mise exec -- ruby -S bundle install
cp config/local.example.json config/local.json
set -gx SESSION_SECRET (mise exec -- ruby -rsecurerandom -e 'print SecureRandom.hex(64)')
mise run serve
```

Open [http://localhost:9292](http://localhost:9292). The local catalogue starts
empty. You can browse it and read the anonymous API. Writes require configured
identities and explicit permissions; there is no local bypass token.

```console
curl --fail http://localhost:9292/health
curl --fail http://localhost:9292/api/v1/services
```

The second request returns `{"services":[]}` before any services have been added.
Stop Puma with Ctrl+C.

## Enable administration and publishing

To work with real entries, configure a trusted browser identity provider and an
explicit bootstrap administrator. Follow <%= link_to 'Manage services and access', '/administration.html' %>
and the <%= link_to 'identity setup checklist', '/operations.html#identity-setup-before-real-publication' %>.
Each installation supplies its own issuer, client configuration and secrets.

For a container, set `databasePath` to `/data/catalogue.sqlite3` in your local
configuration and use `docker compose up --build`. Compose binds only to
loopback; a production instance needs an HTTPS reverse proxy and persistent
storage. See <%= link_to 'Operate an instance', '/operations.html' %>.
